asset management, and banking industries.

234
00:13:41,420 --> 00:13:44,620
So there's definitely been major moves some of these entities have already made in the

235
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:49,580
space, but I expect the big unlock is going to come when there is fully legislated stable

236
00:13:49,580 --> 00:13:51,580
coin and market structure laws.

237
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:55,740
Banks are very risk averse, just like us, just like public accounting firms, and many

238
00:13:55,740 --> 00:13:59,180
are still waiting to see what happens with advancing legislation.

239
00:13:59,180 --> 00:14:03,420
All of that being said, while we are definitely seeing a large increase in activity, I'd still

240
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:07,660
say most of our traditional clients have not meaningfully engaged in the ecosystem yet.

241
00:14:07,660 --> 00:14:09,820
And as the old adage goes, we're still early.

242
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:11,900
Yeah, we're still early.

243
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:14,060
How have attitudes evolved in this respect?

244
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:19,340
Because I probably imagine that the actual strategic moves to integrating crypto workflows

245
00:14:19,340 --> 00:14:21,340
don't mirror where the attitudes stand.

246
00:14:21,340 --> 00:14:22,940
There's usually a lag there.

247
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:28,300
Are you seeing attitudes evolving in that respect among those traditional clients?

248
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:31,740
Or do you still see a post FTX crater of sorts?

249
00:14:31,740 --> 00:14:36,380
Do people view stable coins markedly different from crypto these days, speaking of stable coins?

250
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:39,900
So I find that people don't know what they don't know.

251
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:43,980
When we schedule these sessions with our many audit clients to see what topics they want

252
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:49,100
to hear about blockchain and crypto and digital assets, it's generally not high on that list.

253
00:14:49,100 --> 00:14:54,300
But when we do make our way into talking about it, it's always one of the best received topics.

254
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:56,540
And again, they just don't know what they don't know.

255
00:14:56,540 --> 00:15:01,500
Regarding what you mentioned about that post FTX crater there, I'd say we're almost fully

256
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:07,260
past the post FTX at this point, where it would really put a damper on the attitude

257
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:08,540
towards blockchain for a while.

258
00:15:09,100 --> 00:15:13,980
Even during that really grueling bear market, we actually continue to invest in our capabilities

259
00:15:13,980 --> 00:15:18,780
because we believe in the promise and ethos of the technology along with our clients.

260
00:15:18,780 --> 00:15:22,060
We didn't abandon them during all of that, and they didn't abandon us.

261
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:25,660
And growth across the board is picking up, and interest again is picking up,

262
00:15:25,660 --> 00:15:28,380
especially with the regulatory changes here.

263
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:34,300
Regarding how people differentiate and conflate crypto to blockchain, to Bitcoin, to stable coins,

264
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:39,260
we still have a long ways to go, I'd say, before the masses are educated on those nuances,

265
00:15:39,260 --> 00:15:40,940
but it is heading in the right direction.

266
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:45,660
That's good to hear about the subject matter interest when you guys are touching on that.

267
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:49,980
What are the blockers or potential unlocks to some of those parties that you mentioned

268
00:15:49,980 --> 00:15:52,460
to launching crypto powered products or workflows?

269
00:15:53,100 --> 00:15:57,900
Especially for more of the traditional companies, first they would need a real business need.

270
00:15:57,900 --> 00:16:00,780
So what does crypto or blockchain solve for them?

271
00:16:00,780 --> 00:16:05,580
Most eyes are on efficiencies gained through disintermediation of the transfer of assets,

272
00:16:05,580 --> 00:16:09,260
and I'd say most large entities in the asset management area

273
00:16:09,260 --> 00:16:13,100
are actively looking at this narrative of real world asset tokenization.

274
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:17,020
It's also hard to ignore everything that the payments giants are doing in this space

275
00:16:17,020 --> 00:16:20,220
and how that could innovate on settlement and payments.

276
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:21,100
Very cool.

277
00:16:21,100 --> 00:16:24,700
When it comes to these use cases, payments being a chief one,

278
00:16:24,700 --> 00:16:27,180
how do you feel about permission versus public blockchains?

279
00:16:27,740 --> 00:16:29,100
Yeah, I get that question a lot.

280
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:33,260
So this is definitely a Greg opinion, not necessarily a firm opinion here,

281
00:16:33,260 --> 00:16:35,260
so I want to be sure to clarify that.

282
00:16:35,260 --> 00:16:38,620
But I personally believe there are a lot of powerful attributes

283
00:16:38,620 --> 00:16:40,860
that are inherent in blockchain technology.

284
00:16:40,860 --> 00:16:46,460
But I think the most important attribute that gives these systems value is censorship resistance.

285
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:50,620
With public blockchains, the fact that all you need is an internet connection

286
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:55,980
and you have access to a global financial system with no biases and no counterparty risk,

287
00:16:55,980 --> 00:16:58,300
that's a very powerful and valuable thing.

288
00:16:58,300 --> 00:17:01,500
Public blockchains, I feel, are effectively public goods.

289
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:04,060
And when you talk about permission blockchains,

290
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:08,140
you're effectively removing that very key attribute of censorship resistance

291
00:17:08,140 --> 00:17:09,180
right out of the gate.

292
00:17:09,180 --> 00:17:12,860
And maybe that calls into question the value proposition of the system

293
00:17:12,860 --> 00:17:15,020
compared to a traditional centralized system

294
00:17:15,020 --> 00:17:18,860
based on your typical servers, databases, APIs, etc.

295
00:17:18,860 --> 00:17:22,860
Now, that being said, there are some popular permission to blockchains

296
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:25,740
used by some of the world's largest banks and asset managers

297
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:28,860
that I would actually say are a resounding success.

298
00:17:28,860 --> 00:17:32,780
And I would agree they're providing an important and valuable service.

299
00:17:32,780 --> 00:17:36,220
But I think this was because a result of regulatory entities

300
00:17:36,220 --> 00:17:39,580
historically calling into question if public blockchains

301
00:17:39,580 --> 00:17:43,100
and still safety and soundness concerns in the banking sector,

302
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:47,660
you take that and combine with the fact that it's also really difficult and expensive

303
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:50,620
to create a new system of network service databases

304
00:17:50,620 --> 00:17:53,180
to execute financial transactions across.

305
00:17:53,260 --> 00:17:55,740
And the software underpinning the Ethereum blockchain,

306
00:17:55,740 --> 00:18:00,060
that's tried, true, and tested for executing low settlement financial transactions.

307
00:18:00,060 --> 00:18:02,540
And since that software can be effectively copied,

308
00:18:02,540 --> 00:18:05,420
fit for purpose, and ran in a permissioned environment,

309
00:18:05,420 --> 00:18:08,060
that might be a much easier, less complicated option

310
00:18:08,060 --> 00:18:11,020
than creating a parallel and alternate financial system

311
00:18:11,020 --> 00:18:13,260
that you have to get industry players to agree on.

312
00:18:13,260 --> 00:18:16,220
So long story short, I think they both have a time and place

313
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:18,300
depending on the facts and circumstances.

314
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:21,340
But public blockchains are resoundingly more imperative systems.

315
00:18:21,820 --> 00:18:24,940
Yeah, it's a great point. It's not easy to stand up a blockchain,

316
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:28,380
both to get it working and then to actually get the flywheel going.

317
00:18:28,380 --> 00:18:32,860
I think that's a lot of why you see these token incentive methods employed.

318
00:18:32,860 --> 00:18:35,500
Granted, it's potentially different scope since, like you said,

319
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:36,940
that was maybe a personal view.

320
00:18:36,940 --> 00:18:38,860
But how does this impact your guy's job?

321
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:43,020
KPMG's role that permission versus public blockchain dichotomy.

322
00:18:43,020 --> 00:18:44,540
Well, auditing ledgers is our thing.

323
00:18:44,540 --> 00:18:45,580
Permissions are public.

324
00:18:45,580 --> 00:18:49,340
They have different trade-offs and efficiencies and how we audit them.

325
00:18:49,340 --> 00:18:52,540
They have slightly different processes that we might utilize,

326
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:54,300
but they're structured pretty well.

327
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:57,980
And for the public chains, we have our proprietary set of tools for that.

328
00:18:57,980 --> 00:18:59,740
And even for the permission chains,

329
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:02,300
we always have a way to get and correlate that data

330
00:19:02,300 --> 00:19:03,580
and their internal controls and such.

331
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:07,420
And in a similar vein, how about when it comes to permissioned

332
00:19:07,420 --> 00:19:12,300
or KY seed assets on chain versus permissionlessly transferable ones?

333
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:13,980
I like where you're heading with this question.

334
00:19:13,980 --> 00:19:17,340
So we're actually seeing a lot of recent innovations more broadly

335
00:19:17,420 --> 00:19:21,100
in the crypto space, such as with Uniswap v4 transfer hooks

336
00:19:21,100 --> 00:19:25,020
and Solana token extensions as part of the token 2022 standard.

337
00:19:25,020 --> 00:19:28,220
So for those that don't know, these are effectively smart contracts

338
00:19:28,220 --> 00:19:33,340
that exist on public permissionless chains that allow for KYC processes

339
00:19:33,340 --> 00:19:36,540
and procedures that we'll need to comply with before something like

340
00:19:36,540 --> 00:19:40,700
a transfer function or any smart contract can be called on that asset.

341
00:19:40,700 --> 00:19:44,220
And I think that's a super elegant way to address KYC concerns

342
00:19:44,220 --> 00:19:48,620
while still keeping that base settlement layer of a public blockchain permissionless.

343
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:52,380
And on top of this, there's also a lot of ZK or zero knowledge based proofs

344
00:19:52,380 --> 00:19:55,500
that can be combined with these methods to instill more privacy

345
00:19:55,500 --> 00:19:57,900
alongside those KYC controls.

346
00:19:57,900 --> 00:20:01,740
So as the value of tokenization and tokenized real world assets keeps growing,

347
00:20:01,740 --> 00:20:05,260
I think these advancements are really logical place to build those solutions upon.

348
00:20:05,260 --> 00:20:10,300
Yeah, it's exciting to see crypto become somewhat natively compliant,

349
00:20:10,300 --> 00:20:13,900
having those technologies in place to just make these transfers

350
00:20:13,900 --> 00:20:16,620
more comfortable for real institutional grade users.

351
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:20,620
Are there any frequent flags that come up when specifically auditing crypto companies

352
00:20:20,620 --> 00:20:22,860
or anything to highlight in that regard?

353
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:26,860
I don't know if I would necessarily characterize these as flags,

354
00:20:26,860 --> 00:20:31,180
but I can think of a few issues that we think are important to work through.

355
00:20:31,180 --> 00:20:35,900
So how a client is able to demonstrate existence and rights to digital assets

356
00:20:35,900 --> 00:20:38,940
when they are held with a third party, particularly when the third party

357
00:20:38,940 --> 00:20:43,580
does not have something like a SOC report that provides assurance over its internal controls.

358
00:20:43,900 --> 00:20:46,140
That's a really important issue to work through.

359
00:20:46,140 --> 00:20:49,580
Also, the clients own internal controls over financial reporting,

360
00:20:49,580 --> 00:20:54,540
particularly those that address the completeness and accuracy of financial information,

361
00:20:54,540 --> 00:20:59,340
related party transactions, particularly given the pseudonymous nature of many blockchains.

362
00:20:59,340 --> 00:21:03,100
And of course, we always see the company that has too many spreadsheets.

363
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:06,060
I have a colleague that has said before that often the digital assets

364
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:09,580
aren't the riskiest part of the audit, but rather a client's use of spreadsheets

365
00:21:09,580 --> 00:21:12,780
because of all the things that can go wrong with them without robust controls.

366
00:21:13,340 --> 00:21:15,020
That makes sense. I buy it 100%.

367
00:21:16,940 --> 00:21:21,900
Greg, it's been great learning about you and the firm's involvement and thoughtful directions.

368
00:21:21,900 --> 00:21:24,300
In closing, I want to ask what guidance would you give to

369
00:21:24,300 --> 00:21:27,420
founders just getting started building crypto businesses?

370
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:33,260
Early on, I'd say most of the focus needs to be on building something that has product market fit,

371
00:21:33,260 --> 00:21:37,740
something where you're providing a product or service that others find valuable and worth

372
00:21:37,740 --> 00:21:44,060
paying for. And once you have that idea, maybe a prototype, MVP, etc., then I think it's important

373
00:21:44,060 --> 00:21:48,620
that companies start thinking real early about the various challenges they can run into that

374
00:21:48,620 --> 00:21:53,500
could threaten their business, hinder growth, or instill too much risk. I think companies should

375
00:21:53,500 --> 00:21:59,020
be sure to document everything that they can in a secure fashion, focus on things like SOC-ready

376
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:05,020
IT security, bulletproof KYC and AML processes and sanctions controls, and of course, don't

377
00:22:05,020 --> 00:22:10,140
cheap out on private key control and custody. So the earlier that companies can start thinking

378
00:22:10,140 --> 00:22:13,980
about these things, the easier it's going to get down the line when they become a larger

379
00:22:13,980 --> 00:22:17,420
focus as they scale their business. We'll leave it there. Well, Greg,

380
00:22:17,420 --> 00:22:21,820
thank you for coming on and sharing you and the firm's insights. It's been great to hear about.

381
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:26,060
Yeah, it's been a pleasure. Thanks so much for having me. I appreciate it. It's been fun.

